## ABSTRACT
Ordered porous solid strong bases (OPSSBs) have attracted great research interest due to the excellent performance as heterogeneous catalysts in various reactions. The main obstacle for fabricating OPSSBs is the requirement of high temperature to produce strong basicity on ordered porous materials. For example, the temperatures of \(600{-}650\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) are required for the decomposition of base precursor \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) to basic sites on mesoporous silica SBA-15 and zeolite Y. Such high decomposition temperatures are energy-intensive and harmful to the structure of supports. Herein, we report the fabrication of OPSSBs by utilizing the redox interaction between base precursor and low-valence metal centers (e.g., \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\)) in metal-organic frameworks (MOFs). The base precursor \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) on MIL-101(Cr) can be converted to basic sites entirely at \(300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which is quite lower than those of the conventional thermal conversion on SBA-15 and zeolite Y \((600{-}650\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\). The exploration on decomposition mechanism reveals that the valence change of \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) to \(\mathrm{Cr}^{4+}\) takes place during the conversion of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) to basic sites. In this way, MOFs-derived base catalysts have been synthesized successfully by the host–guest redox strategy and exhibit high catalytic activity in typical base-catalyzed reactions.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks, ordered porous solid strong bases, host–guest redox interaction, transesterification reaction, Cr centers
## 1 Introduction
Solid strong bases are widely investigated heterogeneous catalysts in catalysis community owing to their distinct advantages containing easy separation, high activity, and economic feasibility [1–3]. These advantages make solid strong bases potential for various reactions (e.g., Aldol condensation, Michael addition, transesterification, and Henry reaction) [4–6]. In the past few decades, much attention has been devoted to fabricating solid strong bases of various disordered porous materials such as \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\) by means of high temperature thermal decomposition [7–10]. However, there are only few reports available about producing strong basicity on ordered porous supports to enhance the selectivity and catalytic activity. Basically, the difference between ordered porous solid strong bases (OPSSBs) and disordered porous solid bases lies in the pore structures. Therefore, recently, the excellent shape-selective catalytic properties of OPSSBs have attracted much attention. Due to their ordered microporous structure, zeolite Y was first employed as host to disperse base precursors of \(\mathrm{KNO}_{3}\) [11, 12]. Nevertheless, the obtained materials required high activation temperature to produce basic sites on zeolite Y \((700\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\). The high temperature ultimately caused the damage of framework, and the basicity thus remained relatively low. Mesoporous silica SBA-15 was chosen as a support for the preparation of OPSSBs due to their ordered structure, large surface area, and well-defined pore size [13, 14].
Lately, some representative methods such as grafting amino groups and doping nitrogen were adopted; however, the obtained materials have still depicted weak basicity [15–17]. To achieve a strong base strength, alkali metal oxides (e.g., \(\mathrm{KNO}_{3}\)) have been loaded on SBA-15 to make OPSSBs [18–20]. Unfortunately, the strong basicity reacts with siloxane skeleton at high activation temperature \((600\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\), leading to the destruction of mesoporous structure which weakens the basicity. This may limit the widespread applications of OPSSBs seriously. Hence, it is highly desirable to prepare OPSSBs with ordered porous structure and strong basicity at relatively low temperatures.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have emerged as remarkable ordered porous inorganic–organic hybrid materials built up from organic linkers and metal ion nodes by means of coordination chemistry [21–24]. They have a variety of advantages such as exceptional porosity, adjustable pore size, and diverse functional groups [25–29]. These advantages lead to them being widely used in adsorption, separation, energy storage, sensing, and photochemistry [30–33]. Hence, this makes MOFs as promising materials for the fabrication of OPSSBs [34]. At present, the primary methods for fabricating MOFs-derived solid bases are still the incorporation of different amino groups and grafting of different organic linkers [35–37]. However, the obtained resultant materials always show weak basicity [38–40]. Although MOFs show great potential for acting as the supports to construct
OPSSBs, the generation of strong basicity on MOFs remains pronounced challenges.
In this work, we designed direct preparation of OPSSBs based on MOF through a host–guest redox (HGR) strategy by using the inherent reducibility in MOF. It is well known that the base precursor of nitrate has oxidizability while the metal centers at low valence possess reducibility. Base precursors can be transformed to strong basic sites at relatively low temperatures by the way of redox reaction betwixt host and guest. As a proof of concept, a typical MOF, MIL-101(Cr), was employed as the host, and \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) was used as the base precursor. Interestingly, the transformation of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) to strong basicity on MIL-101(Cr) occurred at \(300\ ^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}},\) which is much lower than that required for the traditional supports such as zeolite Y \((650\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) and SBA-15 \((600\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) (Fig. 1). The decomposition mechanism of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) loaded on MIL\(101(\mathrm{Cr})\) was studied in detail by different methods, indicating that the valence change of \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) to \(\mathrm{Cr}^{4+}\) is the key factor of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) activation at low temperature \((300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) . As a result, MOFs-derived solid strong bases were synthesized successfully by the HGR strategy with ordered porous structure, strong basic sites, and good catalytic activity in base-catalyzed reactions. The catalytic activity is superior to most of the typical solid bases like \(\mathrm{MgO}\) along with a sequence of reported basic catalysts which contain alkali metal and alkaline earth metal oxides.
## 2 Results
## 2.1 Decomposition of the base precursor \(\mathbf{NaNO}_{3}\)
To create strong basic sites, the base precursor \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) was supported on three typical supports with ordered pore structure: zeolite Y, mesoporous silica SBA-15, and MOF MIL-101(Cr). Basically, \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) is expected to convert to \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) with strong basicity. Initially, the decomposition of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) on disparate supports was characterized by thermogravimetry-mass spectrometry (TG–MS). The gas products released from the samples during the heating process were monitored by MS, which can precisely characterize the thermal decomposition behavior of the samples. As shown in Fig. 2, four MS signals were detected with \(m/z\) values of 30, 32, 44, and 46, which are attributed to NO, \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) , \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) , and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) , severally. The signal of NO \((m/z=30)\)
originated from \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) was used to distinguish the decomposition behavior of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) on three aforementioned supports.
It is conspicuous that \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) shows different decomposition ways on different supports. First of all, we can see that the three samples all have different degrees of weight loss peaks at room temperature to \(200\ ^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}},\) which is attributed to the desorption of adsorbed water (Fig. 2). In the case of Na/SBA-15 and Na/MIL\(101(\mathrm{Cr})\) , the weight losses at \(200{-}300\ \,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) are assigned to the decomposition of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) (Figs. 2(b) and 2(c)). As depicted in Fig. 2(a), \(\mathrm{Na/Y}\) reveals a distinct weight loss at about \(650\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) which is attributed to \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) decomposition, indicating that the generation of basic sites on zeolite Y demands high temperature. As for Na/SBA-15 (Fig. 2(b)), partial \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) is converted at about \(400\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) and complete decomposition of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) takes place at 600 \(^\circ C\) . On MIL-101(Cr), the conversion of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) occurs at about \(300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 2(c)), which is obviously lower than that on Y and SBA-15. Further increasing the temperature to \(500\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) the degradation of MOF skeleton occurs. Interestingly, the pyrolysis of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) on zeolite Y principally produces NO and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) as well as a small amount of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) (Fig. 2(a)). In contrast, the chief products are NO and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) as well as a small amount of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) on Na/SBA-15 (Fig. 2(b)). For the sample Na/MIL-101(Cr), NO is the main product with trace of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) from \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) decomposition whereas the gas product of MOF skeleton is \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (Fig. 2(c)). The different gaseous products derived from \(\mathrm{Na/Y}\) , Na/SBA-15 and Na/MIL-101(Cr) indicate different pathways of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) conversion.
Figures 3(a)–3(c) depict the infrared (IR) spectral data of different samples. As exhibited in Figs. 3(a) and 3(b), the loaded \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) on zeolite Y and SBA-15 gives a distinct IR absorption peak at \(1,384~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) belonging to \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\)vibration from \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\). After activation at 300 and \(400\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the samples Na/Y-300, Na/SBA-15-300 and Na/SBA-15-400 still illustrate a strong vibration band of \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\), indicating that \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) is barely decomposed. However, lately, at high temperature, this respective band disappears, confirming the complete decomposition of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) on Na/Y-650 and Na/SBA-15-600. Similarly, from the spectra of MIL-101(Cr) series samples (Fig. 3(c)), a new absorption band of \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\)appears at \(1,384~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) after the introduction of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\). Such an IR band becomes weak after activation at \(300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), proving the pyrolysis of
The X-ray diffraction (XRD) characterization was further employed to analyze the different samples (Figs. 3(d)–3(f)). As shown in Fig. 3(d), a new diffraction peaks emerges at \(2\theta\) of \(29.4^{\circ}\) after the modification of zeolite Y with \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) (Na/Y), which can be ascribed to \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) (JCPDS No. 07-0271). Obviously, the sample \(\mathrm{Na/Y-300}\) still exhibits intense diffraction peaks of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) after pyrolysis at \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), demonstrating the partial decomposition
of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\). The peaks of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) disappear after pyrolysis at 650 \(^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), indicating the basic sites generated at high temperature. However, the original structure of zeolite Y seems broken after being activated at \(650~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 3(d)). As shown in Fig. 3(e), SBA-15 only exhibits an amorphous XRD diffraction peak. After introducing \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\), there is a new diffraction peak at \(29.4^{\circ}\), which can be assigned to \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\). Furthermore, after activation at 300 and \(400\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the samples \(\mathrm{Na/SBA-15-300}\) and \(\mathrm{Na/SBA-15-400}\) still depict intense diffraction peaks of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\), demonstrating incomplete decomposition of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\). In case of \(\mathrm{Na/SBA-15-600}\), the diffraction peak of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) disappears, confirming the complete decomposition of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) at high temperature. As shown in Fig. 3(f), after modification with \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\), the \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) diffraction peak of \(\mathrm{Na/MIL-101}(\mathrm{Cr})\) at \(2\theta\) of \(29.3^{\circ}\) can be observed. Ultimately, the diffraction peaks of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) disappear in \(\mathrm{Na/MIL-101}(\mathrm{Cr})-300\), validating its entire decomposition at relatively low temperature of \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
From the results above, it is apparent that the base precursor
\(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) on MIL-101(Cr) can be completely converted at \(300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) which is remarkably lower than the pyrolysis temperature for \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) on traditional supports such as zeolite Y \((650\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) and SBA15 \((600\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\).
## 2.2 Structural properties of the obtained solid bases
The structure of obtained OPSSBs' was analyzed by different methods. Figures 4(a)–4(f) exhibit \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption–desorption isotherms as well as pore size distributions of different samples. The structural properties including surface area and pore volume are presented in Table S1 in the ESM. In the case of \(\mathrm{Na}/\mathrm{Y}{-}650\) and Na/SBA-15-600, relatively low \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) uptake is achieved, demonstrating the collapse of porous structure (Figs. 4(a) and 4(b)). Emphatically, the BET surface area of \(\mathrm{Na}/\mathrm{Y}{-}650\) is \(134\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , which is much lower than that of zeolite Y \((1,235\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) . Similarly, a decrease from \(1,222\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (SBA-15) to \(35\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for Na/SBA-15-600 is observed. As depicted in Figs. 4(d) and 4(e), the parent sample zeolite Y exhibits a micropore size distribution centered at \(0.6\;\mathrm{nm}\) , and SBA-15 exhibits a mesopore size at around \(8\,\textrm{n m}\) . However, in the case of \(\mathrm{Na}/\mathrm{Y}{-}650\) and Na/SBA-15-600, no obvious pore size centers are detected, signifying the damage to porous structures after activation. On the contrary, the BET surface area of MIL-101(Cr) descends to 2,645 from \(3,492\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after the introduction of basic sites. The shape of the isotherm of \(\mathrm{Na}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}300\) is comparable to that of pristine MIL-101(Cr) (Fig. 4(c)). However, the pore size of \(\mathrm{Na}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}300\) is smaller than that of the parent sample MIL-101(Cr) (Fig. 4(f)), indicating that the basic \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) sites locate in the pore channels.
To characterize and verify the structures of different samples, TEM and SEM micrographs of the samples are obtained and displayed in Figs. 4(g)–4(l), and Figs. S3 and S4 in the ESM. As
To characterize and verify the structures of different samples, TEM and SEM micrographs of the samples are obtained and displayed in Figs. 4(g)–4(l), and Figs. S3 and S4 in the ESM. As shown in Fig. 4(g), on supported zeolite Y, regular structure can be seen from TEM and SEM images (Fig. 4(g) and Fig. S4(a) in the ESM). Nonetheless, the loading and activation of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) obviously change the pore structure and an amorphous structure can be seen in the images of \(\mathrm{Na}/\mathrm{Y}{-}650\) (Fig. 4(j) and Fig. S4(b) in the ESM). In the case of SBA-15, a periodic mesoporous structure is visible from Fig. 4(h) and Fig. S4(c) in the ESM. The sample Na/SBA-15-600 has no parallel channels on the supports, and disordered pore arrangement is observed (Fig. 4(k) and Fig. S4(d) in the ESM). The mesostructure of SBA-15 was further characterized by low-angle XRD technique. As shown in Fig. S5 in the ESM, the original SBA-15 shows similar intense strong diffraction peaks and two relatively weak peaks, in connection with the crystal faces of (100), (110) and (200) [41]. This reflects that the parent sample has an ordered mesostructure. However, for the sample Na/SBA-15-600, no diffraction peak is detected, indicating that the meso-structure is degraded after activation (Fig. S5 in the ESM). In contrast, the original topological structure of MIL-101(Cr) is well-preserved after the introduction of the basic sites (Figs. 4(i) and 4(l), and Figs. S3(b) and S4(f) in the ESM). The EDX-mapping images of \(\mathrm{Na}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}300\) are obtained and shown in Fig. S3 in the ESM. C, Cr, Na and O atoms are detected, which are distributed uniformly in the sample Na/MIL-101(Cr)-300.
From the results mentioned above, it is found that the MOFs-derived solid bases have the original ordered porous structure of MIL-101(Cr). On the other hand, zeolite Y and mesoporous silica SBA-15 show collapsed structure after the decomposition of the base precursor of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\).
## 2.3 Basicity and catalytic performance
The basicity of different samples was measured by titration. The supports MIL-101(Cr), Y and SBA-15 exhibit tiny amount of basic
sites, indicating the negligible basicity. For the sample Na/MIL-101(Cr)-300, the amount of total basicity is \(0.96\;\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\), which is close to the theoretical value \((0.98\;\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}})\), further proving the entire decomposition of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) at a relatively low temperature of \(300\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The samples Na/Y-650 and Na/SBA-15-600 only present 0.33 and \(0.36\;\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) of the amount of basic sites, which is obviously lower than the theoretical value \((0.98\;\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}})\) due to the reaction of strong basic sites with the supports of zeolite and mesoporous silica. The obtained materials were applied as solid catalysts for the transesterification of methanol and ethylene carbonate to synthesize dimethyl carbonate (DMC). As an environmentally green chemical, DMC can be applied as a gasoline additive due to its good blending properties and high octane number (105) [42]. More importantly, DMC is a promising and safer alternative for poisonous phosgene and dimethyl sulphate because of its high biodegradability and low toxicity [43, 44]. Continuous efforts have been devoted to the synthesis of DMC by using various types of catalysts in homogeneous or heterogeneous reaction phase such as metal oxides, hydrotalcite, and polymers. The influence of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) loading on the yield of DMC was first explored. When the loading amount of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) is \(5\;\mathrm{wt.\%}\), the yield of DMC is \(12.3\%\). In the case of Na/MIL-101(Cr)-300 with \(10\;\mathrm{wt.\%}\) of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\), the yield of DMC increases to \(33.6\%\). However, the yield decreases to \(24.8\%\) when the loading further increases to \(20\;\mathrm{wt.\%}\) (Fig. S6 in the ESM). Due to the redox interaction between \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) with \(\mathrm{Cr}\) centers in MIL-101(Cr), the excessive loading of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) leads to the collapse of MOF structure and reduces its catalytic performance (Fig. S7 in the ESM). Therefore, the sample loaded with \(10\;\mathrm{wt.\%}\) of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) was used in further investigations. As shown in Fig. 5(a) and Fig. S8 in the ESM, the support MIL-101(Cr) has no catalytic activity. After activation at \(400\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the samples Na/Y-400 and Na/SBA-15-400 show very low catalytic activity due to the incomplete decomposition of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\). The samples Na/Y-650 and Na/SBA-15-600 also exhibit a low yield of DMC (\(14.8\%\) and \(13.6\%\), respectively). This is mainly caused by the reaction between the strong basic sites and the zeolite/silica skeleton at high temperature, leading to decreased basicity and collapsed structure. These results suggest that the catalysts with better structure show higher activity. It is worth noting that the yield of DMC is \(33.6\%\) over the sample Na/MIL-101(Cr)-300. The introduction of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) on MIL-101(Cr) results in an obvious high catalytic activity. The materials based on zeolite Y and SBA-15 were also activated at a relatively low temperature of \(400\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to preserve the pore structure, and the obtained samples were denoted as Na/Y-400 and Na/SBA-15-400, respectively. The
respective yield of DMC is \(1.7\%\) and \(9.6\%\) over Na/Y-400 and Na/SBA-15-400, due to the incomplete decomposition of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) (Fig. S8 in the ESM). To examine the catalytic stability, the catalysts were recycled after reaction. There is almost no detectable loss in activity for Na/MIL-101(Cr)-300 even after 6 recycles (Fig. S9 in the ESM). This result indicates the good reusability of the obtained catalysts. The samples Na/Y-650 and Na/SBA-15-600 can be also reused for several times, and the structure of recovered catalysts is comparable to those of the fresh ones (Fig. S10 in the ESM).
To further ascertain the catalytic activities of Na/MIL-101(Cr)-300, several reported catalysts used for the synthesis of DMC are listed for comparison (Table S2 in the ESM). Over the benchmark solid base \(\mathrm{MgO/C}\), the yield of DMC is about \(19.6\%\). For the strong solid base catalyst \(\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}/\mathrm{SBA}-15\), \(28.4\%\) of DMC is yielded, whereas the DMC yield is \(43.4\%\) under the catalysis of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{SBA}-15\). It can be seen that Na/MIL-101(Cr)-300 performs better than the majority of solid bases. To compare the efficiency of Na/MIL-101(Cr)-300 with other reported solid bases, we calculated the turnover frequency (TOF) values (Fig. 5(b) and Table S2 in the ESM). The TOF of Na/MIL-101(Cr)-300 is estimated to be \(104.2\;\mathrm{h^{-1}}\), which is higher than those of other popular solid bases such as \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{SBA}-15\) (\(20.3\;\mathrm{h^{-1}}\)) and \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{Cr}-\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) (\(90.1\;\mathrm{h^{-1}}\)) reported previously (Table S2 in the ESM). The excellent catalytic activity of Na/MIL-101(Cr)-300 can be ascribed to well-dispersed basic sites and well-developed porosity, which makes the basic sites highly accessible to the reactants. Apparently, these results validate a good catalytic performance of Na/MIL-101(Cr)-300 as compared with typical traditional solid strong bases that are used for DMC synthesis.
## 3 Discussion
As demonstrated above, OPSSBs can be prepared successfully from MIL-101(Cr) at a relatively low temperature, which is due to the redox interaction between \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) centers with reducibility in MIL-101(Cr) and the \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\)with oxidizability in \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\). The valence change of \(\mathrm{Cr}\) in the redox process was explored. The \(\mathrm{Cr}\) 2p XPS spectra of the samples MIL-101(Cr), Na/MIL-101(Cr) and Na/MIL-101(Cr)-300 are shown in Fig. 6(a). In the cases of MIL-101(Cr) and Na/MIL-101(Cr), two peaks centered around the binding energies of 587.7 and \(577.6\;\mathrm{eV}\) are associated with \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) species [45, 46]. As depicted in Fig. 6(a), after pyrolysis at \(300\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), two new peaks at the binding energy values of 588.6 and \(579.3\;\mathrm{eV}\) appear in the sample Na/MIL-101(Cr)-300 which are assigned to \(\mathrm{Cr}^{6+}\) species [47, 48]. This means that low valence \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) in MIL-
\(101(\mathrm{Cr})\) is oxidized to high valence \(\mathrm{Cr}^{6+}\) during the conversion of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\).
In order to further explore the valence change of \(\mathrm{Cr}\), the diffuse reflectance ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) spectra were further measured to analyze the different samples. As shown in Fig. 6(b), in the case of MIL-101(Cr) and Na/MIL-101(Cr), two wide light absorption peaks appear in the UV center at about 455 and 600 nm, which correspond to \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) species [49–51]. After activation at \(300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), a new UV peak becomes visible at \(230\ \mathrm{nm}\) and the blue shift of the peak at \(250\ \mathrm{nm}\) is observed because of ligand-to-metal charge transfer, which can be attributed to \(\mathrm{Cr}^{6+}\) species in Na/MIL-101(Cr)-300 [52, 53]. At the same time, the peaks of \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) decline distinctly, which demonstrates that partial \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) is transformed into \(\mathrm{Cr}^{6+}\). Furthermore, the consumption of \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) was quantitatively calculated on the account of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) in the MIL-101(Cr) series samples. The results of XPS demonstrate that \(12.2\%\) of \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) is oxidized to \(\mathrm{Cr}^{6+}\) for sample Na/MIL-101(Cr), which is very near to the theoretical value \((10.3\%)\) and confirms that all \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) is reduced to \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{O}\).
The synchrotron-radiation-based X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) and extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) were carried out to demonstrate the electronic structure and coordination information of the \(\mathrm{Cr}\) atoms in materials. Figure 6(c) shows the XANES spectra of MIL-101(Cr) series samples, with the reference spectra of Cr foil, \(\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\). The absorption edge positions of Na/MIL-101(Cr)-300 shift to higher energies, further revealing that a fraction of low valence \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) in MIL-101(Cr) is oxidized to high valence \(\mathrm{Cr}^{6+}\) during the conversion of \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) [54, 55]. This observation agrees with the XPS and UV-vis results. Further Fourier-transformed EXAFS spectra in Fig. 6(d) show that the \(\mathrm{Cr-O}\) (1.54 Å) coordination structure in Na/MIL-101(Cr)-300 is the same as those in the MIL-101(Cr) and Na/MIL-101(Cr) samples, revealing that the MIL-101(Cr) structure is retained [56–58].
The above results indicate that the redox reaction between base precursor and \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) metal atom centers is beneficial to decrease the activation temperature on MIL-101(Cr). \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) is converted to \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) is converted to high-valence \(\mathrm{Cr}^{6+}\) during activation, as demonstrated by several characterization technologies including
XPS, UV–vis, XANES and EXAFS spectroscopy. Quantitative calculation exhibits that \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) in MIL-101(Cr) is adequate to realize the conversion of loaded \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\). The theoretical values are good in accord with the measured ones as per the diverse characterizations carried out. The existence of low valence variable metals in MIL-101(Cr) is the key to the conversion of base precursor to basicity. In theory, this phenomenon can also occur in other MOFs if there are metal centers at low valence such as MIL-53(Cr), MIL-100(Fe) and MIL-47. Work is in progress to introduce basic sites to such MOFs.
## 4 Conclusions
In brief, we have come up with the HGR strategy to manufacture OPSSBs by introducing \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) to MIL-101(Cr), and the original topological structure of MIL-101(Cr) remains intact after the generation of strong basic sites. This approach is superior to the traditional thermal induced pyrolyzation of base precursors. In the pretreatment process, the base precursor \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) is transformed to strongly basic sites of \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) while \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) centers are oxidized to \(\mathrm{Cr}^{6+}\). Therefore, the strongly basic sites can be produced on MIL-101(Cr) at \(300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which is unexpectedly lower than that generated on zeolite Y \((650\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) and SBA-15 \((600\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\). Low temperature activation not only saves energy, but also facilitates the retention of ordered porous structure. It is different from the structural collapse of traditional supports zeolite Y and SBA-15. As a result, MOFs-derived solid strong bases were synthesized successfully with ordered porous structure, strong basic sites and extraordinary catalytic activity in base-catalyzed reactions. The catalytic activity is superior to most of the representative solid base like \(\mathrm{MgO}\) along with a sequence of reported basic catalysts which contains alkali metal and alkaline earth metal oxides.
